@fragment
# DoH bootstrap blocking - INCLUDED BY OTHER PROFILES, not selectable itself.
#
# Browsers that use DNS-over-HTTPS must first resolve their DoH provider's
# hostname using normal DNS. Denying that lookup pushes them back onto the
# system resolver, which is the Pi.
#
# LIMITS - read before relying on this:
#   - A browser configured with a hard-coded DoH IP address never performs a
#     lookup, so there is nothing here to block. This closes the common case,
#     not every case.
#   - Firefox honours the use-application-dns.net canary only in its
#     automatic/default mode. If DoH is switched on deliberately, it ignores
#     the canary completely.
#   - This is DNS denial, not a firewall. The Pi is not the gateway, so it
#     cannot stop outbound traffic to a DoH endpoint on port 443.
# Treat this as a safety net for a browser you forgot, not as permission to
# turn DoH back on.
#
# SAFE FOR THE PI ITSELF: these rules are emitted with $client= scoping for
# bricked devices only. AdGuard's own upstream (dns10.quad9.net) is resolved
# from 127.0.0.1, which is not a bricked client, so the Pi's own resolution
# is unaffected. Verified after deployment.

# --- Cloudflare (Chrome, Edge and Firefox default) ---
cloudflare-dns.com
                        # also covers mozilla.cloudflare-dns.com,
                        # chrome.cloudflare-dns.com, security. and family.
one.one.one.one

# --- Google ---
dns.google
                        # Blocks the DoH endpoint and the hostname used by
                        # Android Private DNS. google.com is NOT affected.

# --- Quad9 ---
dns.quad9.net
dns10.quad9.net

# --- Other common providers ---
dns.adguard-dns.com
dns.nextdns.io
doh.opendns.com
doh.cleanbrowsing.org
doh.dns.sb
doh.pub
dns.alidns.com

# --- Firefox canary ---
use-application-dns.net
                        # Firefox disables DoH by itself when this name fails
                        # to resolve, but only while DoH is left on automatic.
